---
/**
 * A3 — uncertainty experiments.
 *
 * The exhibit is A3-01, the error-budget ranking at three assumed pressure standard
 * deviations. The table is built from the same summary block the figure was drawn from,
 * republished under site/public/data/ and checked against its digest during this build.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Figure from "../../../components/Figure.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/DataTable.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
import { checkedExhibit } from "../../../scripts/exhibits";
import { download, readJson } from "../../../scripts/public-data";
import { NAV, NOT_RUN } from "../../../scripts/site";
import { href } from "../../../lib/urls";

const a3 = await checkedExhibit("A3-01");
const csv = await download("a3_01_error_budget_ranking.csv");
const json = await download("a3_01_error_budget_ranking.json");

interface Ranking {
  rankings_by_assumed_sigma: Record<string, Array<{ source: string; relative_effect: number }>>;
  top_two_by_assumed_sigma: Record<string, string[]>;
  ranking_stable_under_sigma_factor_two: boolean;
  short_depletion_penalty_f010_over_f050: number;
}
const r = await readJson<Ranking>("a3_01_error_budget_ranking.json");

const groups = Object.keys(r.rankings_by_assumed_sigma);
/** Sigma value out of a key such as "sigma_22.5_psia". */
function sigmaOf(key: string): string {
  return key.replace(/^sigma_/, "").replace(/_psia$/, "");
}

/** One row per error source, one column per assumed sigma. */
const sources = [
  ...new Set(
    groups.flatMap((g) => (r.rankings_by_assumed_sigma[g] ?? []).map((e) => e.source)),
  ),
];
const rows = sources.map((source) => {
  const row: Record<string, string> = { source };
  for (const g of groups) {
    const hit = (r.rankings_by_assumed_sigma[g] ?? []).find((e) => e.source === source);
    row[g] = hit === undefined ? "" : `${(hit.relative_effect * 100).toFixed(3)}`;
  }
  return row;
});
/* Order the table the way the study orders it: by the declared programme's sigma. */
const declared = groups.find((g) => sigmaOf(g) === "45") ?? (groups[0] as string);
rows.sort((a, b) => Number(b[declared] ?? 0) - Number(a[declared] ?? 0));

const contents = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "evidence", label: "Evidence" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What this does not establish" },
  { id: "reproduce", label: "Reproduce and download" },
];

const description =
  "A3 is an ordinate-side measurement error budget on a synthetic closed tank. Its headline ranking is not robust, which was pre-registered as an inconclusive condition and reported as one.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="A3 — uncertainty experiments"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/studies/a3/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Study A3 — supporting work</p>
  <h1>The error budget that re-ranks itself</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Given a realistic survey programme on a synthetic closed-tank dry-gas reservoir, which
    error source dominates the inferred gas in place? At the declared programme — twelve
    annual shut-in surveys, 50 percent of the gas produced by the last one, an assumed
    45 psia standard deviation on the reported average reservoir pressure — random pressure
    scatter is largest at 1.32 percent of G and a 25 psia shared calibration bias is second
    at 0.86 percent. <strong>That ranking is not robust.</strong> Halving the assumed scatter
    reverses the top two.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Synthetic throughout.</strong> {NOT_RUN.fieldData}
  </p>

  <section id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-h">
    <h2 id="overview-h">Overview</h2>

    <p>
      The reversal was pre-registered as an inconclusive condition and the condition
      triggered. The ranking of the top two is therefore reported as a restatement of an
      assumed number, not as a result: the assumed pressure standard deviation is something
      this study did not measure and could not measure, because the reservoir is synthetic.
      Everything below the top two is stable across the factor of four in assumed sigma that
      was tested, and those positions are reportable.
    </p>

    <h3>What does not depend on the assumption</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <em>level</em> of the deviation factor is exactly irrelevant to a p/Z gas in
        place. Over a grid of uniform multiplicative errors in Z running to plus and minus 5
        percent, the largest movement of the x-intercept is 3.1e-16 relative — machine zero,
        not a small number. That is algebra, and it is the one result here that transfers to
        any reservoir. What moves G is the <em>shape</em> of Z across the observed window:
        a 1 percent tilt moves G by 1.09 percent on this reservoir.
      </li>
      <li>
        A shared offset behaves qualitatively differently from scatter: it does not shrink
        with more surveys. At +25 psia the bias is +0.8624 percent at 12 surveys and +0.8655
        percent at 48, while the random one-sigma falls from 1.32 to 0.70 percent. Past about
        30 surveys the bias is the larger term and no further surveying touches it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Supplying psig where psia was required makes the reservoir look <strong>smaller</strong>,
        by 0.504 percent here — 1.58 times the common rule of thumb, a factor that is a
        property of this Z(p) over this window and not a constant.
      </li>
      <li>
        Short observed depletion is the strongest design lever examined: at fixed noise,
        fixed survey count and an evenly spaced abscissa, the one-sigma error at 10 percent
        depletion is {r.short_depletion_penalty_f010_over_f050.toFixed(2)} times the error at
        50 percent. At 4 percent depletion the exact confidence set is unbounded 74 percent of
        the time, so the correct engineering output there is a lower bound rather than an
        error bar.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <Callout kind="note" title="Prospective ordering, recorded exactly">
      <p>
        This study's run script declares its protocol as an input and its run record pins it
        by SHA-256, so the protocol content that existed at run time is provably the
        committed content. That establishes <em>internal content linkage as recorded</em>. It
        is not an independent trusted timestamp and not proof against retrospective
        construction: no signed tag and no trusted timestamp attests to it, so every date in
        it is self-reported.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-h">
    <h2 id="evidence-h">Evidence</h2>

    <Figure
      id={a3.id}
      number={a3.number}
      title={a3.title}
      svg={a3.svg}
      units={a3.units}
      interpretation={`Only the random-scatter row moves with the assumed gauge accuracy, and it moves far enough to swap the top two: the ranking is stable under a factor of two in the assumption — ${r.ranking_stable_under_sigma_factor_two ? "yes" : "no"}.`}
      caveat={a3.caveat}
      longDescription={a3.longDescription}
      evidenceClass={a3.evidenceClass}
      tableLabel="Data table — the ranking at three assumed sigmas"
      tableOpen={true}
      svgDownload={a3.svgDownload}
      dataDownload={json.path}
      dataDownloadLabel={json.name}
    >
      <DataTable
        caption="Effect on G as a percentage of G, at the base survey programme. Ordinate sources only: the abscissa is absent from this table because it was never perturbed. Rows are ordered by the declared 45 psia column."
        captionTitle="Table A3-01"
        columns={[
          { key: "source", label: "Error source", rowHeader: true },
          ...groups.map((g) => ({
            key: g,
            label: `Assumed sigma ${sigmaOf(g)}`,
            unit: "% of G",
            numeric: true,
          })),
        ]}
        rows={rows}
        id="t-a3-01"
      />
      <Fragment slot="footnote">
        <p>
          Only the first row moves. At the declared sigma the order is scatter, then bias,
          then the psig slip, then the correlation choice, then the standard basis, then
          nothing; at half that sigma the top two swap. Three further honesty points travel
          with the table. The 25 psia calibration bias is a <em>chosen</em> magnitude,
          justified as 0.25 percent of full scale for a 10,000 psi gauge, so a different gauge
          class gives a different row. The correlation-choice row is the spread between
          published correlations, which is not the same quantity as the error against
          laboratory PVT, so it is a lower bound on the deviation-factor risk rather than an
          estimate of it. And the whole table is conditional on the tank model being right: a
          weak aquifer biases G by more than every row of this table added together, while{" "}
          <em> improving</em> the apparent straightness of the line — which is what study
          {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a4/")}>A4</a> demonstrates.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </Figure>

    <h3>The next measurement, with its break-even</h3>
    <p>
      Of four candidate next steps compared on a stated convention — the one-sigma random
      standard error and the absolute value of the systematic shift added <strong>linearly</strong>,
      because a bias is not a random variable and adding it in quadrature would understate it
      — a calibration and datum check on the pressure surveys gives the lowest projected total
      error, 1.32 percent against 1.52 to 1.84 percent for the others. That conclusion assumes
      the check leaves no residual datum error: a residual above about 5.83 psia would put it
      behind the next-best option. No cost or effort model is part of this study.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
    <h2 id="limits-h">What this study does not establish</h2>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="The headline ranking is inconclusive by its own criterion">
      <p>
        The top two sources swap when the assumed pressure standard deviation is halved. That
        was pre-registered as an inconclusive condition, the condition triggered, and it is
        reported as inconclusive rather than resolved in favour of the declared programme.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="The budget is ordinate-only">
      <p>
        Cumulative production is never perturbed anywhere in this study. A metering bias would
        enter the answer one for one, so "what dominates the uncertainty in G" is answered for
        the pressure side of the line and not for the volume side. The study's own
        recommendation for the repository is to fix that next.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="No interval treatment survives a drifting gauge">
      <p>
        The delta method, the Fieller set and the bootstrap all fail at a correlation of 0.8
        in the pressure error, with coverage falling from 0.94 to 0.54 for both interval
        methods.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="Conditional on a tank model nothing here tests">
      <p>
        A weak aquifer would break the whole budget by more than every measured source
        combined, and the usual field arguments would not settle it: low water production does
        not establish a closed tank, and neither does a p/Z estimate that looks stable.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="limitation" title="One correlation-risk figure comes from a study that cannot be reproduced here">
      <p>
        The comparison of correlations against laboratory-grade reference equations belongs to
        study <a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>A2</a>, which reads a reference extract this
        repository does not redistribute. Those figures cannot be reproduced from this
        checkout and nothing in this study's results depends on them; the correlation-choice
        row above is a correlation-against-correlation spread, computed here.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section id="reproduce" aria-labelledby="reproduce-h">
    <h2 id="reproduce-h">Reproduce, and download the numbers</h2>

    <pre><code>PYTHONPATH=src python3 cases/A3_uncertainty_experiments/run.py \
    --out artifacts/A3_uncertainty_experiments/run-008</code></pre>

    <p>
      Python standard library only, single declared seed, about fifteen seconds. The shipped
      run is <code>run-006</code>, whose committed snapshot this site's figure was drawn from.
    </p>

    <ul class="downloads">
      {[csv, json].map((d) => (
        <li>
          <a href={d.href} download>
            {d.name}
          </a>
          <span class="downloads__meta">
            {d.size} — {d.description}
          </span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    .downloads {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .downloads li {
      padding-block: var(--space-2);
      border-block-end: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
    }
    .downloads__meta {
      display: block;
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--c-ink-2);
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
